-- title:
--   Radical and unique prime support of finite contact ideals
-- statement:
--   Let $g_2,g_3\in\mathbb C$, let $A=\mathbb C[t,x_1,x_2,x_3]$, and choose either of the elliptic-extension chart derivations
--
--   $$D_0=\partial_t+x_2\partial_{x_1}+(6x_1^2-g_2/2)\partial_{x_2}-x_1\partial_{x_3},$$
--
--   $$D_1=\partial_t+(-6x_2^2+(g_2/2)x_1^2)\partial_{x_1}+(-1/2-g_2x_1x_2-(3g_3/2)x_1^2)\partial_{x_2}+(-2g_2x_2^2-3g_3x_1x_2)\partial_{x_3}.$$
--
--   Write $D=D_c$. For each $v\in\mathbb C^4$ and $n\in\mathbb N$, let $C(v,n)$ be the chart contact ideal generated by polynomials whose first $n$ $D$-jets vanish at $v$.
--
--   Let $V\subseteq\mathbb C^4$ be any finite set and let $n_v\in\mathbb N$ for $v\in V$. Define
--
--   $$I=\bigcap_{v\in V}C(v,n_v),\qquad \mathfrak m_v=\ker(\operatorname{ev}_v:A\to\mathbb C).$$
--
--   Then
--
--   $$\sqrt I=\bigcap_{\substack{v\in V\\n_v>0}}\mathfrak m_v.$$
--
--   Moreover, for every prime ideal $P\subseteq A$,
--
--   $$I\subseteq P\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\text{there is a unique }v\in V\text{ with }n_v>0\text{ and }P=\mathfrak m_v.$$
--
--   Thus the primes containing $I$ are exactly the distinct point maximal ideals with positive contact order.
--
--   **Formalization Note** This is unconditional for the defined chart derivations and contact ideals. It uses the already proved contact structure theorem; no extra contact-membership, primarity, dimension, time-presentation, or first-coordinate injectivity hypothesis is imposed. Distinct full coordinate vectors can have the same first coordinate. The points need not lie on the chart cubic. Empty $V$ and zero contact orders are included. The formal radical formula keeps all indices and assigns the whole ring to zero-order factors, which is equivalent to the displayed intersection over positive orders. If all orders vanish, $I=A$ and no prime ideal contains it. The chart indices 0 and 1 correspond to the homogeneous-coordinate charts $S_0$ and $S_2$. The result identifies prime support of a finite contact ideal; it gives no global degree or algebraic-group estimate.
-- source:
--   Derived finite-contact support calculation for the commutative-algebra approach associated with Senthil Kumar K (2026), Appendix A, https://doi.org/10.1017/S001309152610145X. Uses the proved elliptic_extension_contact_ideal_structure. Radical commutes with finite intersections, a prime containing a finite intersection contains a factor, and its point radical is maximal. Coordinate polynomials distinguish point evaluation kernels and give uniqueness. Not quoted as Theorem A.2. Primary formal references: Ideal.radicalInfTopHom, map_finset_inf, Ideal.IsPrime.inf_le', Ideal.IsPrime.radical_le_iff, RingHom.ker_isMaximal_of_surjective and Ideal.IsMaximal.eq_of_le.

import Definitions.Def_WeierstrassEllipticZeta_ProjectiveContactIdeal
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Operations
import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.Maximal

open WeierstrassEllipticZeta
open scoped Classical

theorem WeierstrassEllipticZeta.finite_contact_prime_support (g₂ g₃ : ℂ) (c : Fin 2)
    (V : Finset (Fin 4 → ℂ)) (n : V → ℕ) :
    let I := ⨅ v : V, extensionChartContactIdeal g₂ g₃ c v.val (n v)
    I.radical = (⨅ v : V, if n v = 0 then ⊤ else RingHom.ker (MvPolynomial.eval v.val)) ∧
    ∀ P : Ideal (MvPolynomial (Fin 4) ℂ), P.IsPrime →
      (I ≤ P ↔ ∃! v : V, 0 < n v ∧ P = RingHom.ker (MvPolynomial.eval v.val)) := by sorry
